\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sa160\sl252\slmult1\i\f0\fs22 Waste Management Recycling Education Representative Bobby Bell shared that he had the opportunity to present to seniors at the Joslyn Community Center last Tuesday, where he provided recycling tips and answered questions; and reminded the community that the final Free Paper Shredding event of the year will be held at Mira Costa High School on October 18, 2025, from 9:00 AM to 11:00 AM.\line\line Police Chief Rachel Johnson invited the community to attend the upcoming Town Hall meeting at the Joslyn Community Center on September 18, 2025, from 6:00 PM to 8:00 PM.\line\line Library Manager Cassandra Sargent invited the community to attend several upcoming events, beginning with tomorrow\rquote s in-person program \ldblquote Why Dolphins Surf?\rdblquote  at 6:00 PM; announced the New Parent Engagement program, scheduled for September 22, 2025, which will introduce new parents to the library\rquote s programs and services; and the Susie Hansen Latin Trio Band will perform on September 23, 2025.\line\line City Manager Talyn Mirzakhanian announced the appointment of the City\rquote s new Community Development Director and introduced Masa Alkire, who then provided brief remarks.\line\line Community Development Director Masa Alkire expressed his excitement about joining the City and shared that he looks forward to serving the community in his new role. He noted that he previously served as the City Planner Assistant Director of Community Development for the City of Beverly Hills, and prior to that, held positions with the City of El Segundo and worked as a consultant.\line\line\pard Mira Costa High School students Ryan Beaupain, Founder and President of the Digital Philanthropy Club, and Luke Finigan, Vice President, shared that they established the club with a mission to recycle e-waste and support youth education in underserved communities. They noted that the club has grown to over 25 members and has engaged many Mira Costa students in volunteer efforts. Last year, the club expanded its outreach to Manhattan Beach Middle School, Pacific Elementary School, and Robinson Elementary School. They proposed hosting an e-waste collection event at the Manhattan Beach Library on November 8, 2025, and responded to questions from the City Council.\cf1\i0\f1\par
}


